<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml">
  <title>Education and Academic Achievement in Adolescence</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00055</md:content-id>
    <md:uuid>0e745a8d-0a98-408f-b510-217f6ffcf53c</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="learning-objectives" id="sect-00001">
    <title>Learning Objectives</title>
    <para id="para-00001">By the end of this section, you will be able to:</para>
    <list id="list-00001">
    <item>Explain how formal education supports cognitive development in adolescence</item>
    <item>Describe trends in academic achievement across adolescence</item>
    <item>Describe how academic achievement varies based on contextual influences</item>
    </list>
    </section>
    <para id="para-00002">Keely teaches first period science in an urban high school. Many of her students travel long distances on public transportation to get to school; none are happy about how early they need to get up to be there. Keely finds these sleepy teens need to get out of their seats in the morning, so she hands them large cards with letters drawn on them. A quick review of the prior week’s lesson on the transcription of DNA sets the stage for what follows: half the students line up in front of the chalkboard while another student pretends to be messenger RNA, ferrying the students to the (mostly) appropriate corresponding bases at the front of the room. Forty minutes after the class has started—and just five minutes before it ends—the students have managed to replicate a single strand of DNA. While the “protein” breaks down and students noisily return to their seats, the Keely recaps what they learned.</para>
    <para id="para-00003">In every post-industrial society, formal schooling is a prominent feature of adolescent life. Not only can such societies afford to set aside the significant time period of adolescence for schooling, but the specialized workforce depends upon a well-educated population of young adults. By 1917, all states in the United States had compulsory education laws, requiring children to attend school until at least age 16 (Hine, 1999; Goldsin &amp; Katz, 2003). Schools became spaces where children not only mastered academic subjects but also learned what was expected of them as citizens. Schooling also paved the way for the development of youth culture and the invention of the term “teenager,” especially with the 1938 passage of the Fair Labor Standards Act, which included extensive federal provisions limiting child labor.</para>
    <para id="para-00004">Today, schools in developed nations like the United States provide a space for adolescents to explore dozens of subjects in the arts, humanities, sciences, and mathematics, as well as vocational training and college preparation. However, that may not be the case worldwide as there are many societies in which adolescents do not have an opportunity for formal education, and instead are expected to join the military (e.g., Afghanistan, Myanmar, Iraq) or help in the farms after they hit puberty (e.g., Brazil, India, China); some teens even get married (e.g., Chad, Niger, Bangladesh to name a few). In this section, you’ll consider how formal schooling affects cognitive development in teens, review some trends in academic achievement during adolescence, and learn how culture plays a role in education and academic performance (<link target-id="fig-00001"/>.</para>
    <figure id="fig-00001">
    <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
    <!-- <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/OSX_Lifespan_09_05_Students.jpg"/>  -->
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/FPO.jpg"/>
    </media>
    <caption>Caption placeholder</caption>
    </figure>
    <section id="sect-00002">
    <title>Schooling and Cognitive Development</title>
    <para id="para-00005">The scientific study of child and adolescent development has provided the theoretical and research basis for modern educators to develop school curricula. Thus, many current classroom practices closely track major shifts in developmental psychology across the 20<sup>th</sup> century. The contemporary classroom is an eclectic mix of approaches, but it generally applies the theory called <term id="term-00001">constructivism</term>, which states that knowledge must be built upon prior knowledge an individual already possesses.</para>
    <para id="para-00006">Constructivism has been called the third metaphor in <term id="term-00002">educational psychology</term> – replacing the behaviorist view of learning as response strengthening, and the 1960s and 1970s view of learners as information processors (Mayer, 1996; Ebo, 2018). It has its origins in cognitive developmental theory, which says that individuals actively construct an understanding of the world (Brainerd, 2003; Fosnot, 1996; Mayer, 1996, 1998; Phillips, 1995, 2000). In other words, factors such as children’s prior experiences, learnings, social and cultural beliefs, and way of processing information allow them to digest knowledge in their own manner. Knowledge is not simply passively received, absorbed, or acquired through imitation. Rather, the learner encounters, addresses, and experiences it. Incoming information is infused with meaning, often uniquely personal, and stored in memory as a knowledge structure or framework constantly subject to revision.</para>
    <para id="para-00007">Learning is thus a dynamic process in which a child is actively engaged and participates in a classroom. There are many variations of constructivism, though, and they often differ significantly with respect to the roles that student and teacher are believed to play in the knowledge construction process, the extent to which learning is embedded in a social context. For instance, in the Montessori approach the emphasis is on hands-on learning and independent exploration within a nurturing and supportive environment. Montessori education encourages children to use their imaginations and be creative. This helps them develop a love of learning and fosters their natural curiosity (Lillard et al., 2021).</para>
    <para id="para-00008">Children and teens spend a considerable part of the day in school. We have long recognized that the relationships they form there are consequential for their personal, academic, and social development (Longobardi et al., 2016; Pakarinen et al., 2018). In fact, once children begin their educational careers, their relationships with their teachers become increasingly important for their success in school (Heatly &amp; Votruba-Drzal, 2017).</para>
    <para id="para-00009">Teachers should allow maximal time for students to reflect on their learning to aid in the organization and transfer of acquired knowledge. This is consistent with the learner-centered principle of metacognition, which states that a learner should be aware of and understand their own thinking processes. The classroom should also be a place where students are free to converse in the language of ideas – to argue, challenge, and concur with one another.</para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00003">
    <title>Academic Achievement in Adolescence</title>
    <para id="para-00010">During adolescence, youth undergo significant cognitive development, including improvements in abstract thinking, problem-solving abilities, and critical reasoning skills. Therefore, educational materials and requirements should be appropriately challenging to stimulate cognitive growth without being overwhelming. Goodness of fit in education refers to the compatibility or alignment between the educational environment and the needs, abilities, and characteristics of individual students. We know that students come from diverse backgrounds, learning styles, and developmental stages; thus, an optimal learning experience would occur when these factors are appropriately matched with the educational context. For instance, offering a variety of class times and modalities (on-ground versus online) to accommodate diverse learning preferences and personal circumstances. Additionally, class times should be structured to accommodate these differences, incorporating breaks and varied activities to maintain engagement and prevent fatigue.</para>
    <para id="para-00011">[Preview H3s]</para>
    <section id="sect-00004">
    <title>Outcomes</title>
    <para id="para-00012">What kinds of outcomes in academic achievement does schooling produce? The high school graduation rate for the United States overall was 86% in the 2018–2019 school year (NCES, 2021). This number has increased consistently since it was first tracked at the national level in 2011. Group-level differences were observed, however. For Asian-American/Pacific Islander students, the rate is 93%, for White students 89%, for Hispanic students 82%, for Black students 80%, and for Native American students 74%.</para>
    <para id="para-00013">The U.S. Department of Education’s National Center for Education Statistics (NCES) produces “The Nation’s Report Card,” a biannual snapshot of fourth-, eighth-, and twelfth-grade achievement in mathematics and reading, among other subject areas. The most recently available data for twelfth grade are for 2019, one year prior to the onset of the COVID-19 pandemic. For mathematics, 60% of twelfth-grade students met or exceeded the basic competence level nationwide, while just 24% met or exceeded what the Center calls a “proficient” level of achievement. This represented a slight decrease in achievement compared with each assessment dating back to 2009. Males and females achieved at roughly similar levels, although female twelfth-graders averaged 3 points behind males on the 300-point scale.</para>
    <para id="para-00014">There were significant mathematics achievement gaps between students from different ethnoracial groups, as well as for students from households with different parental levels of schooling, and also for students who were English-language learners. Black students showed a 31-point gap in mathematics achievement compared with White students; Hispanic students were 22 points behind White students; Asian-American students showed a 14-point gap in their favor when compared with White students (<link target-id="fig-00002"/>). Students whose native language was not English showed the largest mathematics achievement gap – 41 points below native English speakers. Finally, the greater the educational attainment of parents, the higher the mathematics achievement of their children; adolescent children of college-educated parents scored 28 points higher on average than children whose parents did not graduate from high school. This finding is expected given that educational attainment is one of the main components of socioeconomic status, an indicator of the level of social (and economic) resources a person or family has at their disposal. Having said that, it is imperative that government provide access to high-quality early childhood education programs to help level the playing field for students from disadvantaged backgrounds. Also, addressing disparities in disciplinary actions, promoting diversity among educators, and providing culturally responsive curriculum and instruction can foster academic success of the marginalized and disadvantaged students. Finally, socioeconomically disadvantaged students often demonstrate remarkable resilience and perseverance, and are highly motivated to succeed academically with the goal to improve their circumstances (Anyaka, 2017). For instance, previous studies have shown that resilient African American students see parental support, regular conversations about the importance of education and college, and their parents’ expectations for them to attend college as key influences (Harper et al., 2012).</para>
    <figure id="fig-00002">
    <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
    <!-- <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/OSX_Lifespan_09_05_MathReport.jpg"/>  -->
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/FPO.jpg"/>
    </media>
    <caption>Caption placeholder</caption>
    </figure>
    <para id="para-00015">By twelfth grade we see similar patterns emerging for reading achievement. The NCES reports slight declines in reading achievement levels between 2015 and 2019 for male students and for those whose parents had some education beyond high school. Otherwise, scores and achievement gaps between groups were stable for twelfth graders across that time period. Of particular note is the gender gap in reading achievement, where females outscore males by 10 points at the end of high school. Other highlighted gaps include a 25-point difference in favor of students whose parents had graduated from college versus students from homes where the parents did not finish high school; a 55-point achievement gap between native English speakers and English-language learners; and a 32-point gap in reading achievement between White and Black students.</para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00005">
    <title>Factors in Achievement Disparities</title>
    <para id="para-00016">Black teens are aware of the racialized nature of achievement disparities. They are frequently reminded of these disparities in their schools as well as on social media (Hope et al., 2015). However, a recent longitudinal study noted that adolescents’ structural attributions (i.e., systemic, and institutional factors that contribute to disparities in academic outcomes between different racial and ethnic groups) for racial achievement gaps increased from Grade 10 to Grade 12 (Bañales et al., 2020). In other words, when Black students recognize that such factors are rooted in historical and ongoing patterns of discrimination, segregation, and inequality within educational systems and broader society, it works as an adaptive mechanism for their psychological development. When perceiving the causes of achievement disparities as external rather than internal, they see that their ability level is not fixed.</para>
    <para id="para-00017">The onset of the COVID-19 pandemic in March 2020 created major disruptions in</para>
    <para id="para-00018">schooling for children in the United States, particularly for socially and economically vulnerable groups (Bansak &amp; Starr, 2021; Bailey et al., 2021; Maldonado &amp; DeWitte, 2022). First, parents from low-income families were more likely to be essential workers exposed to the COVID-19 virus, and less likely to have access to high-quality healthcare (Berube &amp; Bateman, 2020). Additionally, these families were less likely than wealthier families to have reliable internet access and computers at home, which made it harder for their children to participate in online education (Stelitano et al., 2020).</para>
    <para id="para-00019">Children in low-income families are also more likely to join public schools that lack the resources needed to support teachers in delivering high-quality online instruction. Additionally, parents with low income are less able to provide extra instructional support through private tutoring and other enrichment activities (Lee et al., 2021).</para>
    <para id="para-00020">Research from China and Indonesia indicates that students in rural regions experienced more negative compared to those in urban areas after school closures during the pandemic (e.g., Pasani et al., 2021). In the Netherlands, Engzell and colleagues (2021) found that primary school students faced declines in math, spelling, and reading progress. The losses were notably larger—up to 60% more—among children from low-income households with less parental education (<link target-id="fig-00003"/>). Thus, the academic achievement of school students was disrupted globally by the pandemic, and specifically, families with low to middle income were hit the hardest.</para>
    <figure id="fig-00003">
    <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
    <!-- <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/OSX_Lifespan_09_05_Reading.jpg"/>  -->
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/FPO.jpg"/>
    </media>
    <caption>Caption placeholder</caption>
    </figure>
    <para id="para-00021">Ability grouping, also known as tracking, is a practice in education where students are placed into different classes or groups based on their perceived academic abilities or achievement levels. This practice involves assigning students into “tracks” based on their prior achievement or ability level (Loveless, 2013), such as “honors,” “regular,” or “remedial” classes, with each track offering varying levels of curriculum difficulty and academic rigor. The primary goal of this placement is to create a more uniform learning environment, allowing teachers to tailor their instruction more effectively to students’ needs and enabling students to benefit from interactions with their peers (Steenbergen-Hu et al., 2016). However, it can promote educational inequalities, perpetuate stereotypes, and reinforce self-fulfilling prophecies, wherein students internalize and conform to expectations associated with their assigned track; especially, seen among ethnic minority students (e.g., Francis et al., 2017; Peterson et al., 2016). Furthermore, such segregation among students based on “ability” contributes to achievement gaps between different social groups, as <term id="term-00003">stereotype threat</term> disproportionately affects marginalized students who are targets of negative stereotypes. Stereotype threat refers to the phenomenon where individuals experience anxiety or fear of confirming negative stereotypes about their social group, which can impair their performance in academic or other achievement-related tasks (Spencer et al., 2016).</para>
    <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00001">
    <para id="para-00022">It turns out stereotypes can affect you—whether you believe in them or not. Watch this <link url="https://openstax.org/r/104Stereotypes">video on stereotypes</link> to learn more.</para>
    </note>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00006">
    <title>Interventions</title>
    <para id="para-00023">Of note is a large-scale intervention undertaken in the United States in the 1950s and 1960s, namely the racial integration of schools (Pickren, 2004; Stanley, 2017). In <emphasis effect="italics">Brown v. Board of Education</emphasis> (1954), the U.S. Supreme Court ruled held that “separate but equal” educational access and services were inherently unequal and ordered an end to racial segregation in the U.S. education system. By the mid-1970s, the first Black students who were young children at the time of the original ruling and its reaffirmation 10 years later showed dramatic increases in academic achievement on national standardized tests (<link target-id="fig-00004"/>).</para>
    <figure id="fig-00004">
    <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
    <!-- <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/OSX_Lifespan_09_05_Gaps.jpg"/>  -->
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/FPO.jpg"/>
    </media>
    <caption>This line graph shows the white-black achievement gap, wherein there is a sizable shrinking of the racial gap in the early period between 1954 and 1974 but little change across the last two decades (from 1980 to 2000). [Hanushek et al. (2019)]</caption>
    </figure>
    <para id="para-00024">What this highlights is that large-scale improvements in social status and access to resources, along with a general push toward leveling the socioeconomic playing field, can have a big impact on learning for minority subgroups. This evidence points a path forward – as societies address equity gaps in a variety of domains of life, we should see generational increases in achievement levels. Over time, group-level achievement gaps should close (<link target-id="fig-00005"/>.</para>
    <figure id="fig-00005">
    <media alt="Alt text placeholder">
    <!-- <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/OSX_Lifespan_09_05_Achieve.jpg"/>  -->
    <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/FPO.jpg"/>
    </media>
    <caption>This line graph illustrates a notable increase in the average achievement level for all teen students, rising by about 0.3 standard deviations over the past 50 years, or roughly 0.06 per decade. However, this trend varies by the age of the student; for example, there has been no improvement in the performance of older students born after 1970.</caption>
    </figure>
    <para id="para-00025">The <emphasis effect="italics">Brown</emphasis> decision is an example of a large-scale, society-wide intervention. Educational psychologists also focus on identifying individual-level psychological factors that have some bearing on educational outcomes. Some key ones include self-determination theory (Ryan &amp; Deci, 2017), expectancy-value theory (Harackiewicz et al., 2016), the stereotype threat process (Spencer et al., 2016), the concept of self-efficacy (Bandura, 1997; Schöber et al., 2018), and the collection of achievement motivation ideas currently known as growth mindset (Dweck, 2017). Across these diverse psychological processes and applications, one theme emerges – that a student’s level of achievement motivation is strongly sensitive to the social environment. Adolescent learners in particular are highly attuned to the achievement-related messages they receive from their peers, teachers, parents, and broader cultural attitudes. You’ll learn about these social aspects of academic achievement in our next chapter</para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00007">
    <title>Familial and Sociocultural Factors</title>
    <para id="para-00026">The role of family, peer, and cultural influences on academic achievement trajectories is multifaceted and complex as a myriad of factors influence students’ beliefs, behaviors, and experiences. At the familial level, we have the SES, parental education level, parental involvement as well as parental expectations. For example, the level of parental involvement in a child’s education, including monitoring homework, attending school events, and communicating with teachers, is strongly associated with academic success (Harper et al, 2012).</para>
    <para id="para-00027">At the social level, school climate, disciplinary practices, peer pressure and influence of academic motivation are some of the forces that can drive a teen’s academic achievement trajectory. School climate is defined as “the quality and character of school life,” which is derived from “patterns of people’s experiences of school life and reflects norms, goals, values, interpersonal relationships, teaching, learning and leadership practices, and organizational structures” (Cohen et al., 2009, p. 182). And research shows that a positive school climate helps mitigate the negative contribution of poor SES background on academic achievement (Berkowitz et al., 2017).</para>
    <para id="para-00028">A longitudinal natural experimental study conducted in Los Angeles high schools from 2013 to 2018 found that students who experienced authoritative school environments during their 10<sup>th</sup> grade year, characterized by high levels of support and structure, exhibited higher levels of self-efficacy and grit in subsequent years. Additionally, these students reported fewer depressive symptoms, less hopelessness, and reduced stress at school and about the future in the 11<sup>th</sup> grade. The study highlights the significant association between school climate, particularly authoritative environments, and improved social-emotional health among adolescents. It suggests that interventions focusing on enhancing relationships with teachers and implementing supportive disciplinary practices may be beneficial for promoting the social-emotional well-being of students (Wong et al., 2021). Additionally, one’s cultural capital, cultural values and beliefs about education, and cultural identity impacts academic performance and achievement trajectory. Specifically, cultural resources such as language, knowledge, values, and traditions passed down within communities can influence students’ academic achievement (e.g., Andersen &amp; Jaeger, 2015).</para>
    <para id="para-00029">School absenteeism (i.e., temporary periods of unexcused school absence) and permanent school dropout are both complex issues influenced by various risk factors that can occur at the individual, family, social, school, and community levels. According to the National Center for Education Statistics (2018), 13% of the 8<sup>th</sup> graders, 14% of the 10<sup>th</sup> graders, and 15% of the 12<sup>th</sup> graders were absent at least three days a month, and 6, 5, and 6% were absent at least five days a month, respectively. In a comprehensive report from the U.S. Department of Education (2016), findings revealed that students with disabilities exhibited higher rates of chronic absenteeism compared to their peers without disabilities. Similarly, Native American and Pacific Islander students displayed a greater tendency for chronic absenteeism when compared to students from other racial and ethnic backgrounds. Additionally, non-English language learner (ELL) students were found to have a higher likelihood of chronic absenteeism in comparison to their ELL counterparts (Garcia &amp; Weiss, 2018).</para>
    <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00002">
    <para id="para-00030">Several years ago, Cleveland Public Schools discovered that over half of their students were persistently absent, missing at least 10 percent of school. Although they have made consistent efforts to tackle the issue, similar to many school districts nationwide, there is still considerable work to be done. Watch this <link url="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ELacS6N7ZGc">video on how positive family support can help reduce chronic school absences</link> to learn more.</para>
    </note>
    <para id="para-00031">Understanding the reasons for why teens drop out of high school is fundamental to improving intervention efforts to promote graduation. Recently, through a meta-analysis evidence was gathered on the risk factors for school absenteeism and dropout (Gubbels et al., 2019). For school absenteeism, numerous risk factors were identified such as negative attitudes towards school, substance abuse, both externalizing and internalizing issues in adolescents, and limited parent-school involvement. In terms of dropout risk, risk factors including a history of grade retention, low IQ or learning difficulties, and poor academic achievement were demonstrated significant risk factors impact (Gubbels et al, 2019). In addition, earlier research found extreme parental permissiveness to be associated with higher rates of dropping out (Rumberger et al., 1990). In a similar vein, researchers conducted a survey with young adults who discontinued their education prior to completion (N = 1,047; age range = 18–25 years). The study aimed to evaluate ongoing stressors in respondents’ lives and identify the primary factors they perceived as leading to their decision to leave school. Through qualitative analysis, the researchers identified seven categories of influential factors including mobility, family dynamics, peer influences, school engagement and environment, health issues, involvement in crime, and a combination of multiple factors. Most respondents indicated that their decision to drop out was associated with issues related to school engagement and the overall school environment (McDermott et al., 2019). Hence, addressing the risk factors for school absenteeism and dropout require a comprehensive, multi-faceted approach that involves collaboration between schools, families, communities, and policymakers.</para>
    </section>
    </section>
    <section class="summary" id="sect-00008">
    <list id="list-00002">
    <item>Formal schooling plays a crucial role in teen development by providing structured learning environments that foster cognitive, social, and emotional growth. Through interactions with peers and teachers, teens develop academic and communication skills, as well as a sense of identity. Moreover, formal education equips adolescents with knowledge and skills necessary for future academic and career success.</item>
    </list>
    </section>
